ferment-note-extrapolation = Part of this prediction relies on estimates outside the measured data: watch the dough more than the clock.
ferment-note-osmotolerant = With { $sugar } % sugar, regular yeast slows down a lot: an osmotolerant (sugar-tolerant) yeast works better above about 10 %.
ferment-note-very-short = Under 45 minutes: the yeast's start-up delay matters and the prediction is rough.
ferment-note-before-fridge = The dough would be ready before the fridge and over-ferment there: shorten the room phase or use less yeast or starter.
ferment-note-no-dose-slow = No reasonable amount reaches the endpoint that fast at these temperatures: allow more time or a warmer dough. Shown: the largest amount.
ferment-note-no-dose-fast = Even a very small amount gets there before that time: use a cooler dough or a fridge phase. Shown: the smallest amount.
ferment-note-rich-cold = Rich doughs and heavily yeasted preferments rise much less in the cold than this predicts: judge them by feel and aroma.
ferment-how = How it is calculated
ferment-how-model = Yeast and the bacteria of a starter speed up with temperature until about 35 °C, following curves measured by Gänzle and colleagues. For yeast, the time scales with the amount as fitted on a chart of 918 pizza-dough times; for sourdough, the dough grows from the share of flour already fermented in the starter, which reproduces The Sourdough Framework's 8–12 hour bulk at its recommended doses.
ferment-how-endpoint = The dough's own temperature is followed through each phase: a dough moved to the fridge keeps fermenting for hours while it cools, and that part often matters more than the fridge time itself.
ferment-how-range = Starters and yeasts differ by up to three times between kitchens: the range covers that until you log your own bakes.
